{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.347893Z","id":"05be61b7b887a750f51f6cc4eb620a08738e947eeff371a305d449490044eb20","body":{"detail":"Failure: live, with a task selected whose memory is being written (so each poll's `await this.inspect()` issues board/memory and board/written reads), click a time to stand at an earlier instant. The worker answers messages in order, so the inspector reads posted during the poll queue behind the seek; the seek's render calls `update()` (memory.js:286-291), which nulls `rams`/`detail`/`inspected` and calls `poll(true)` -- but `this.busy` is still true, so `poll` returns at line 305 without setting `this.again` (only `zoom()` sets it, line 452). The in-flight `inspect()` then stores `inspected` with bytes read at the new instant but `now` of the old one, the poll ends with `again` false, and because a page standing at an instant is not re-rendered (app.js render() only runs on worker state messages), `update()` is never called again: `drawMaps` keeps returning early on an empty `shown()` (line 465), so the live canvases stay on screen and the header is not redrawn. A second variant: if the writes read itself is answered after `update()` reset `rams`, line 323 builds fresh all-zero pictures and applies only the cells written since the old `this.now` (the `since` computed at line 309), and the `answer.now < this.now` reset at 317 is skipped because `rams` is null -- so the picture is nearly blank and, standing, nothing corrects it. The initial `board/tasks` read (262-270) has the same shape: it never checks that `board.image` is still the image it was read for.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":253},"end":{"line":349},"content_hash":"6d76a38a3c150e44f8d1e1b1e19ae509b948704eb99a5a2e44c775db00dd644c"},"suggested_fix":"Give each read a generation: bump `this.generation` in `update()` whenever the instant or image changes (and in `zoom()`), capture it at the top of `poll()`/`inspect()`/the tasks read, and discard any answer whose generation is stale. In `update()`, when `this.busy` is true and the instant or image changed, set `this.again = true` so the `finally` block re-polls once the stale poll unwinds.","summary":"Standing at another instant while a poll is in flight drops the re-read, so the memory panel freezes on the previous instant's picture","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.347979Z","id":"83bfeb157a3e332a15ffcfa326d0da091c0ace0708e92c5bb2c48a7ee0d0afef","body":{"detail":"Failure: click a task with a large statics range (up to INSPECTED = 64 KiB, i.e. 16 sequential pairs of board/memory + board/written reads, lines 858-866), then before it finishes click another task with a small range. `pick()` (776-785) and the owner click (962-969) each set `this.inspected = null` and call `this.inspect()` directly, while a poll may also be awaiting `inspect()`. Nothing checks, after the awaits, that `key` still matches the current `this.selected`/`this.focus`: the second call finishes first and draws, then the first call assigns `this.inspected = { key: <old>, ... }` (line 867) and `drawInspector()` renders the old task's statics under the new task's name (`owner` at line 920 reads `this.selected`). Live, the next poll notices the key mismatch and re-reads; standing at an instant, no poll runs again (see app.js render(), only called on worker state), so the wrong statics stay.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":827},"end":{"line":869},"content_hash":"233123ac2e734d2158f3e9dc7c6ae3612f02a947b81b25aca854c6efecb495d9"},"suggested_fix":"After each `await` in `inspect()`, recompute the current key (or compare a per-call token stored on `this`) and return without assigning `this.inspected` if it has changed; or serialize inspector reads through a single in-flight promise that is re-run when the selection changes.","summary":"Concurrent inspect() calls race: a slower read for the previous selection overwrites the inspector for the new one","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348030Z","id":"45db3fbf76b54e01bf3c9187408010e5f63dde32f6f6b3ebf4aef708cd244d3f","body":{"detail":"Failure: a board whose configuration names no archive (or whose archive fails to load). board/tasks then answers `{\"archive\":null,\"error\":\"the board's configuration names no archive\"}` (crates/kernel/src/board.rs tasks_json, around line 1110). The handler at memory.js:264 returns on `tasks?.error` without setting `this.tasks`, which `update()` had just set to `null` (line 261); the `.catch` likewise leaves it null. `drawOwners()` (line 804) chooses its message by `this.tasks === null`, so it always prints \"reading the board's archive...\" and never the \"no archive says what this memory is\" text, and the server's error string is never shown. The periodic re-read at line 275 is gated on `this.tasks` being truthy, so it is never retried either.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":262},"end":{"line":270},"content_hash":"70c36fed0aa1abf8c8465c9ddca9b98bec6244c00e5a6f9e8daf6127105ed171"},"suggested_fix":"On an error answer store it (e.g. `this.tasks = { error: tasks.error }` or a separate `this.tasksError`) and have `drawOwners()` show that error instead of the loading text; reserve the loading text for the window before the first answer arrives.","summary":"A board with no archive shows 'reading the board's archive...' forever; the intended 'no archive' message is unreachable","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348078Z","id":"6c0eafd8bb54ebae4b8c5afe241518058118d178f5de50d915fca02c01212f8f","body":{"detail":"On `answer.now < this.now` the code sets `this.busy = false` and does `return this.poll(true)` inside the try. In an async function a `return <promise>` inside try does not wait for the promise before running `finally`, so the inner poll synchronously sets `busy = true`, hits its first await, and then the outer `finally` (line 342) sets `this.busy = false` while the inner poll is still reading. Any `update()` 200 ms later starts a second poll alongside it (both with `rams` null, so both issue since=0 full reads, and both run `inspect()` concurrently), defeating the single-flight guard at line 305.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":316},"end":{"line":348},"content_hash":"75531f3dd80508abcb22236d3b44f245746a399e81ecf3c06522cf5c73bb337b"},"suggested_fix":"Do not recurse from inside the try: set a flag (e.g. `this.again = true`) and return, letting the existing `finally` branch start the fresh poll after `busy` is cleared; or `return await this.poll(true)` and drop the manual `this.busy = false`.","summary":"When the board goes back, the restarted poll runs with busy cleared, so polls can overlap","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348114Z","id":"9f2d1b008422155687430d26fc4cce43267154d562f2f28cd15a4511b46220c7","body":{"detail":"`drawOwners()` computes `lastWrite(o.ranges[0].base, o.ranges.at(-1).end)` (line 808), and `inspect()` reads from `Math.min(...bases)` to `Math.min(base + INSPECTED, Math.max(...ends))` (847-848). For an owner whose writable sections or stack are not contiguous -- a Hubris task with more than one RAM region, e.g. a network task with buffers in a second SRAM, or a kernel whose stack and data are in different RAMs -- the glow dot labelled 'how lately each wrote' lights for writes anywhere between the two regions (other tasks' memory, whole RAMs in between), the inspector re-reads whenever any of that is written, it fetches up to 64 KiB of bytes that belong to no static shown, and statics in the higher region past 64 KiB from the lowest base are dropped behind 'the first 64 KiB shown'. Also `ranges.at(-1).end` is not the maximum end when ranges overlap or nest.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":805},"end":{"line":848},"content_hash":"f2811f18797d811ccf05aa18c623928832ebd735d8cebec44c013cfce92fab09"},"suggested_fix":"Compute the glow as the max of `lastWrite(r.base, r.end)` over each range, and have `inspect()` read each static's (or each contiguous cluster's) bytes separately rather than one [min, max) span, keeping per-cluster base offsets for `wordOf`.","summary":"Owner glow and inspector span from an owner's lowest range to its highest, including other owners' memory in between","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348151Z","id":"678fa817b1aecbb816ab68a38011c3c9b4ffd8b98a16641a721078d6588cb93f","body":{"detail":"Each poll ends with `await this.inspect()`; when the key and last write are unchanged it still calls `drawInspector()` (lines 850-853), which regenerates a span per byte for every static row (16 bytes each, 1024 when opened, across up to 64 KiB of statics) with heat colours and 'N ago' texts that change every time `this.now` advances. Because the HTML string always differs, `set()` hands it to app.js `update()`, which parses it into a template and walks the entire inspector DOM in `morph()` five times a second, on the main thread, alongside a full `paint()` of up to 65536-cell canvases with two extra `label` passes over every cell. For an owner with hundreds of statics this is tens of thousands of nodes compared per poll and competes with the board panel and timeline for the same thread.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":850},"end":{"line":927},"content_hash":"c93343ac56fb5eace030a236e10922704b447608d040af6584d0e8a0689d61fd"},"suggested_fix":"Separate structure from heat: render the rows only when `inspected`, the sort, the mark filter or `open` change, and on a plain tick update just the changed background colours/ages (e.g. by iterating the `[data-w]` spans, or by drawing byte heat to a small canvas per row). Also skip `paint()` when no stamp changed and heat has dropped below the visible threshold.","summary":"The inspector's whole HTML is rebuilt and morphed on every 200 ms poll, even when nothing it shows was written","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348191Z","id":"36cef528f99bfb342252b6345ef39060766ebf65e6f6fe80ecb926f2cfc9960e","body":{"detail":"`wordOf` (line 876) indexes words from `s.base & ~3`, anticipating an unaligned base, and chunk boundaries line up because every chunk start is `base + k*4096`. But board/written aligns its address down and returns `length.div_ceil(4)` words (crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1399-1400), not `(length + (address & 3)).div_ceil(4)`. For the last chunk with `at % 4 = r` and `(end - at) % 4 + r > 4` (e.g. base 0x20000101, end 0x20000109), the final word is missing: `s.words[w]` is `undefined`, the byte renders `cold`, and a write landing only in that word is missed by the row's last-write scan (lines 892-897). Today the base is usually a stack's `deepest` (word aligned), so this bites owners without a stack whose lowest static is byte-aligned, and the `focus` path is aligned to 256.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":858},"end":{"line":866},"content_hash":"8a887b24b68ca03c97a1f4af405d150bfc4b489f57076d01f76161ae2d43bee6"},"suggested_fix":"Either align the inspected range in the client (`base &= ~3`, `end = (end + 3) & ~3`) before chunking, or fix written_json to return `(length + (address & 3)).div_ceil(4)` words; add a test for an odd base.","summary":"With an unaligned inspected base, the last word of the range is never fetched, so its bytes show as never written","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.test.mjs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348218Z","id":"7f25e46b7eb167d8515d75bb2f73cdf06fb02faacb3bec703a7c59d4a56d24ce","body":{"detail":"The comment says 'A block past the end of the RAM is no block: the run as it is', but `rounded(0x38800e00, 0x180, ram)` returns the in-RAM aligned block {0x38800e00, 0x200} -- the length grew from 0x180, so it is the ordinary rounding case -- and `rounded(0x387fff00, 0x200, ram)` is a run that starts below the RAM, not a block that overhangs its end. The fallback branch for a run inside the RAM whose aligned block overhangs it (e.g. rounded(0x3003f000, 0x2000, {base: 0x30000000, length: 288*1024}), whose smallest aligned block is 512 KiB) has no assertion, nor does `zoomFor`'s `whole` test that decides whether the 'whole block' button appears.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":93},"end":{"line":96},"content_hash":"0f645690ab716503b74a558a45aee759409ff19ca70999091158a551a1a4279c"},"suggested_fix":"Replace the first assertion's comment or add `assert.deepEqual(rounded(0x3003f000, 0x2000, { base: 0x30000000, length: 0x48000 }), { base: 0x3003f000, length: 0x2000 })`, and keep the below-base case labelled as such.","summary":"The 'block past the end of the RAM' case is not actually tested","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348257Z","id":"d41434cb664b1fba21633b5a4641a0eef47f40e1c65a348f5edc86ca340ba969","body":{"detail":"The tests cover `layout`, `tiles`, `rounded` and the refinement property, but the arithmetic that turns a dragged box into a zoom -- refining/coarsening rects by 2 per 4x cell change (703-712), the run's first/last cell and `countIn`-based `whole` test (714-730), the block's bounding rect at 4 B (736-754), and the `sqrt(cell / picture.cell)` scaling of a rect at one grain onto a canvas stretched by a non-integer factor (761-773) -- is only reachable through a canvas and getBoundingClientRect. A regression here (e.g. an off-by-one in the coarsening `ceil`) would not be caught by `node --test web/memory.test.mjs`.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":576},"end":{"line":774},"content_hash":"7b3cbf829ba0ef1bfb39c8624be8fdece390175207a78ae28cb36d83ddf0102f"},"suggested_fix":"Extract `zoomFor(picture, rect, cell, layoutOf)`, `blockRect(view, block, ramBase)` and a pure `rectOnBox(rect, cell, picture, boxW, boxH)` as module functions taking plain data, have the class call them, and add tests: zooming the whole 288 KiB RAM yields cell 16 and a 2x rect, a block's rect at 4 B is a square/2:1 of exactly block.length/4 cells, and a block rect mapped at a coarser grain lands on whole picture cells.","summary":"The zoom geometry (cellAt, boxed, zoomFor, blockZoom, onCanvas) lives in the DOM class and has no tests","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/index.html","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:45:02.348286Z","id":"46ea34a7a791418bd155b804f9eceecc44d494d11fe36668a316d5aecf424cc2","body":{"detail":"`.mem-crumbs` is `white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis`, and drawCrumbs (memory.js:429-440) puts every crumb (each with a name and a 0x...-0x... range), then the 'whole block' button, then the hint on that one line. Two or three zooms deep, or in a panel narrower than ~900px, the button is cut off and cannot be clicked, and nothing else offers it.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":146},"end":{"line":146},"content_hash":"88158b7dbd28afd280b20ee5c439a405d7aaba18a8b10ee4d4ab643e61ccfc2e"},"suggested_fix":"Let the crumb line wrap (`flex-wrap: wrap` or `white-space: normal`), or render the block button and hint on their own line ahead of the crumbs.","summary":"The crumb line clips with ellipsis, hiding the 'whole block' button on narrow panels or deep zooms","tags":["review","review:memory-visualizer","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870604Z","id":"4612f5e69c9c4101dcf26e52ea1aa98af5ccba4d5b1adf195a9eac9c0e79aa6a","body":{"detail":"web/memory.js:286-291 update() nulls rams/detail/inspected and calls poll(true); poll returns at 305 when this.busy, and this.again is set only by zoom() (452), so finally (342-347) does not re-poll. update() is only reached from renderers.board (web/app.js:1366), which runs on worker 'state' messages (app.js:352-355) and panel clicks; seekBoard (app.js:110-118) calls stop(), so standing produces no further state messages. drawMaps returns early when shown() is empty (memory.js:465). Second variant holds: since is captured at 309 before the await, and the answer.now<this.now reset at 317 requires this.rams non-null; 323 then rebuilds from a delta read. Same unchecked image at 262-270.","kind":"comment","references":"05be61b7b887a750f51f6cc4eb620a08738e947eeff371a305d449490044eb20","summary":"confirmed: a seek during an in-flight poll drops the re-read and the standing panel never re-polls","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870633Z","id":"f6452042677db07160f49b1929af1186fcbbaa4353a397c4fbafe725b6eda334","body":{"detail":"web/memory.js:776-785 (pick) and 962-969 (owner click) call this.inspect() directly while a poll can also be inside it (poll line 339). inspect() computes key at 832, awaits board/symbols (836) and up to INSPECTED/SERVED = 65536/4096 = 16 sequential memory+written pairs (858-866, constants 208-209), then assigns this.inspected = { key, ... } at 867 with no comparison against the current this.selected/this.focus; drawInspector (920) labels it with this.selected. Standing, no further poll runs (update() only from app.js:1366 on state messages; seekBoard calls stop()), so the stale statics persist.","kind":"comment","references":"83bfeb157a3e332a15ffcfa326d0da091c0ace0708e92c5bb2c48a7ee0d0afef","summary":"confirmed: inspect() never re-checks its key after awaiting, so an older slower read overwrites the newer selection","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870644Z","id":"f28614f18ec46834a8daba67f76e3f212adfb945e7e82f0e6896ee1959ec247c","body":{"detail":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1109-1110 answers {\"archive\":null,\"error\":...} with no archive or a failed load. web/memory.js:261 sets this.tasks = null, 264 returns on tasks?.error without assigning, .catch at 270 likewise. drawOwners (804) prints \"reading the board's archive…\" whenever this.tasks === null and the owners list is empty; the only path that sets this.tasks (265) is the success path, which also fills owners, so the 'names none' branch is unreachable. The periodic re-read (275) requires this.tasks truthy, so it never retries.","kind":"comment","references":"45db3fbf76b54e01bf3c9187408010e5f63dde32f6f6b3ebf4aef708cd244d3f","summary":"confirmed: an archive-less board shows the loading text forever; the 'no archive' message cannot be reached","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870653Z","id":"1b6b4e5dd004e6bed46e1596dc4580fb3cdabed76fe5c637d97e6555237a6458","body":{"detail":"web/memory.js:317-321 sets busy=false and returns this.poll(true) inside try; finally at 342 then sets busy=false. Node check (scratch script modelling the same shape: outer clears busy, returns inner poll() from try, finally clears busy; inner awaits 50 ms) printed 'busy while inner poll in flight (10ms): false' and a second call was admitted. So the busy guard at 305 is defeated until the inner poll ends. Concern severity is appropriate.","kind":"comment","references":"6c0eafd8bb54ebae4b8c5afe241518058118d178f5de50d915fca02c01212f8f","summary":"confirmed: return this.poll(true) inside try lets finally clear busy while the restarted poll is still reading","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870660Z","id":"7c60b082519c51e4054c25089e474f6b427f680f946c72d3581b4fe54d9c86db","body":{"detail":"owners() sorts an owner's writable sections plus stack by base (web/memory.js:89-91). drawOwners calls lastWrite(o.ranges[0].base, o.ranges.at(-1).end) (808), and lastWrite (401-409) scans every RAM cell between those bounds, other owners' memory included. inspect() reads Math.min(bases) to min(base+INSPECTED, Math.max(ends)) (846-848) in one span. Sorted by base, ranges.at(-1).end is not the max end when an earlier range extends further. Concern severity is appropriate.","kind":"comment","references":"9f2d1b008422155687430d26fc4cce43267154d562f2f28cd15a4511b46220c7","summary":"confirmed: the glow and inspector use one [lowest base, highest end) span per owner","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870667Z","id":"1fef7376c3ab47ef6dc93c3ae0148ad5745a79870af313388c85d9424077aca6","body":{"detail":"poll ends with await this.inspect() (web/memory.js:339); inspect returns this.drawInspector() even when key and last are unchanged (850-853). drawInspector emits a span per byte with a heat colour and age(this.now - last) text (877-912), so the string differs whenever now advances, and set() (1009-1012) hands it to app.js update() (memory.js:216 shown = update), which parses a template and walks it in morph() (app.js:1554-1574). Polls run every POLL_MS = 200 (memory.js:205). Performance concern; severity fits.","kind":"comment","references":"678fa817b1aecbb816ab68a38011c3c9b4ffd8b98a16641a721078d6588cb93f","summary":"confirmed: each poll rebuilds the whole inspector HTML and morphs it, because its 'N ago' text changes with now","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870675Z","id":"776bf2fa69b59f10cdaa4ff77c9d51fe76420572936328a9302bdc09ea420e43","body":{"detail":"crates/kernel/src/board.rs:1398-1399: address &= !3; words = length.min(MEMORY_SERVED).div_ceil(4). For address 0x20000101, length 8 it returns 2 words (0x100-0x107) while byte 0x108 needs a third. web/memory.js:876 wordOf indexes from s.base & ~3, so s.words[w] is undefined for that byte, renders cold (881), and the row's last-write scan (892-897) misses it. Non-final chunks line up (each starts at base + 4096k, so the next chunk's aligned start follows directly).","kind":"comment","references":"36cef528f99bfb342252b6345ef39060766ebf65e6f6fe80ecb926f2cfc9960e","summary":"confirmed: written_json returns length.div_ceil(4) words from the aligned-down address, so an unaligned last chunk misses its final word","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.test.mjs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870681Z","id":"d74a24af26ba6b47e57898e69d1b24989d6a1a148e5a096f9ef82051bf0cbbf5","body":{"detail":"web/memory.test.mjs:93-96. rounded() (web/memory.js:171-180): rounded(0x38800e00, 0x180) with no RAM also returns {0x38800e00, 0x200}, so the first assertion is plain rounding; the second starts below ram.base. Node check: rounded(0x3003f000, 0x2000, {base:0x30000000, length:0x48000}) returns {base: 0x3003f000, length: 0x2000}, the untested fallback the finding names. No test imports or exercises zoomFor (test imports at memory.test.mjs:4 are cellOwners, heat, hilbert, layout, owners, rounded, shortName, tiles). `node --test web/memory.test.mjs`: 9 pass.","kind":"comment","references":"7f25e46b7eb167d8515d75bb2f73cdf06fb02faacb3bec703a7c59d4a56d24ce","summary":"confirmed: the 'past the end of the RAM' comment labels assertions that exercise other cases","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870689Z","id":"07aaee9b56d202508af545e0af9e11d9de683b302f49bc1a22cae113672ac026","body":{"detail":"cellAt (web/memory.js:579), boxed (679), zoomFor (700), blockZoom (736) and onCanvas (761) are MemoryView methods; web/memory.test.mjs:4 imports only cellOwners, heat, hilbert, layout, owners, rounded, shortName, tiles, and none of its 9 tests touches them. Suggestion severity fits.","kind":"comment","references":"d41434cb664b1fba21633b5a4641a0eef47f40e1c65a348f5edc86ca340ba969","summary":"confirmed: the zoom geometry methods are class members over canvases and have no tests","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/index.html","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:48:26.870695Z","id":"e9bcf3594769d5e680c004a087e895c636136a5ab0b22699f94109f626587688","body":{"detail":"web/index.html:146 .mem-crumbs { white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis }. web/memory.js:428-440 puts every crumb (name plus 0x..–0x.. range) and then the data-block button (inside an inline-flex .controls span, index.html:139) and the hint on that one line, so the button is the first thing past the right edge. One caveat: Shift-drag (hint text, memory.js:439) can also zoom to an aligned block, but nothing else offers the current zoom's whole block in one click. Suggestion severity fits.","kind":"comment","references":"46ea34a7a791418bd155b804f9eceecc44d494d11fe36668a316d5aecf424cc2","summary":"confirmed: the crumb line is nowrap with overflow hidden, so a long line clips the 'whole block' button","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/index.html","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504385Z","id":"7c5db28a62100e84cdc5bfe88c343474e4c8bd02f16492126f97c9e2d5e9680c","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: .mem-crumbs wraps (display flex, flex-wrap: wrap) instead of clipping, so the whole-block button stays reachable (web/index.html).","supersedes":"46ea34a7a791418bd155b804f9eceecc44d494d11fe36668a316d5aecf424cc2","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504411Z","id":"20656919607868950783f8fb3e469d4776946cbb507a50b528da373488c7944c","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: MemoryView keeps a generation bumped on image, instant and zoom changes; poll/inspect/nameStatics drop answers from an older generation and set `again`, and update() sets `again` when the instant changes while a poll is in flight (web/memory.js). Browser check (headless Chromium against web/demo/hubris.wasm): selected hiffy, stood at a write, stepped back 100 µs — \"after standing: canvases 6 | statics 18\", \"stepped back: canvases 6 | statics 18\", errors: none.","supersedes":"05be61b7b887a750f51f6cc4eb620a08738e947eeff371a305d449490044eb20","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504434Z","id":"fbb6f2129f15f570af1643fd09e5fa4949f25bff15b660a26acaeb8560e55a03","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: readTasks() stores an error answer as this.tasks = { error } and drawOwners shows \"nothing says what this memory is: <error>\"; the periodic re-read is gated on this.tasks being set, so a board whose archive appears later is picked up (web/memory.js).","supersedes":"45db3fbf76b54e01bf3c9187408010e5f63dde32f6f6b3ebf4aef708cd244d3f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504455Z","id":"cf5d2b145ed7d9e24571c844d4c25a828904617a34c0ab751ae26482a52dfe41","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: on the board going back, poll() clears its pictures and sets `again` instead of recursing, so the finally block starts the fresh poll after busy is cleared (web/memory.js poll).","supersedes":"6c0eafd8bb54ebae4b8c5afe241518058118d178f5de50d915fca02c01212f8f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504477Z","id":"3eb68568d917dd3aaedca7f830c5553444917a460ae4338b2fe157b5c6fac2e6","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: refine, coarser, runOf, rectAround, rectOnBox and clusters are module functions the class calls; web/memory.test.mjs tests each (node --test web/memory.test.mjs: 14 passed).","supersedes":"d41434cb664b1fba21633b5a4641a0eef47f40e1c65a348f5edc86ca340ba969","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504499Z","id":"4415cc94c56c9ada501d4facdb3733593ae487541aeb5e253f4460644f742fe5","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: the owner glow is the max of lastWrite over each of its own ranges, and inspect() reads each cluster of statics separately (clusters(), gap 256 B, 64 KiB in all), so memory between an owner's regions is neither read nor watched (web/memory.js drawOwners, inspect).","supersedes":"9f2d1b008422155687430d26fc4cce43267154d562f2f28cd15a4511b46220c7","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504523Z","id":"5f7decd9a32a3de65192e37bee54689abc09e5cfbe2faf3149fb037ae9b989d2","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: inspect() takes a token (this.inspecting) and the generation, and returns after any await if either changed, so a slower read for an earlier choice never assigns this.inspected (web/memory.js inspect). Browser check (headless Chromium against web/demo/hubris.wasm): selected hiffy, stood at a write, stepped back 100 µs — \"after standing: canvases 6 | statics 18\", \"stepped back: canvases 6 | statics 18\", errors: none.","supersedes":"83bfeb157a3e332a15ffcfa326d0da091c0ace0708e92c5bb2c48a7ee0d0afef","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504548Z","id":"56be4128a07817907021f8bf9ce9a16ce24fb15c317d532c5fb7b0acfe4dbf93","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: drawInspector rebuilds rows only when their shape (what was read, sort, filter, open, mark, focus) changes, otherwise warm() updates byte backgrounds and ages in place; drawMaps runs only when cells were written, heat is still fading, or a click asked (web/memory.js). Browser check: long tasks 0.","supersedes":"678fa817b1aecbb816ab68a38011c3c9b4ffd8b98a16641a721078d6588cb93f","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504574Z","id":"89441dda2227af0e7cadf49f0a09264193168ad0826c0fbf15328682f9cc523d","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: clusters() aligns each run to whole words (test \"statics are read in clusters, word aligned, and only so much\"), and written_json returns every word the bytes touch, (length + (address & 3)).div_ceil(4) (crates/kernel/src/board.rs, git:5f3e6d9).","supersedes":"36cef528f99bfb342252b6345ef39060766ebf65e6f6fe80ecb926f2cfc9960e","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/memory.test.mjs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-27T12:59:09.504600Z","id":"6443563f1cc19cd6f4ed76d0f70e1a8978c8cf01536736a740269eaba9c5fdae","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:c852755","summary":"fixed: web/memory.test.mjs now asserts the overhang case rounded(0x3003f000, 0x2000, SRAM1-3) is the run as it is, and labels the below-base case; and the whole-block decision is covered via runOf's count (test \"a box's run is its cells' first and last, and how many it holds\").","supersedes":"7f25e46b7eb167d8515d75bb2f73cdf06fb02faacb3bec703a7c59d4a56d24ce","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/screen.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:03.272902Z","id":"6592aff4f7ab6f8b8c8fba924846b1ca0f8484e521f4f048f17ed1b87c537013","body":{"detail":"On the Hubris bench, which has no LTDC, this reads board/display every frame for nothing. A complete version would start polling only when the board state says it has a display.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":91},"end":{"line":104},"content_hash":"720987d382ccf2841099308da83cc4ad8f7743b0c00676f4e181e5def38fd73f"},"summary":"The screen polls with requestAnimationFrame whether or not the board has a panel","tags":["session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720236Z","id":"ee4375010de69f0fb24baaa0721e3b55361ce10428502365d886c8af93d4c4e1","body":{"detail":"Failure: a store S at pc 0x100 (x = x + 1) whose previous window shows x set by other code at pc 0x200 from x's own held value (x = x & 0xff, i.e. store(0x200) <- merge(and) <- held(x, written by 0x100)). The first splice correctly puts the 0x200 store in as 'began from' and returns the held under it (line 206). goOn goes on from that held; recurrence() (lines 160-167) climbs past the role:'before' 0x200 store to S and samePlace(S, held) is true because the held was last written by 0x100, so the next splice folds again: S.repeats goes up and the 'before' target (the 0x200 store) is overwritten by the earlier window's base (line 193). Reproduced with goOn over three canned windows (S=10 <- held 9; S=9 <- store(0x200,8) <- and <- held 0x108; S=0x108 <- held 0x107): the final tree is 'S = 0xa x3, began from 0x107', which is arithmetically impossible (0x107 + 3 != 0xa) and no longer mentions the 0x200 write at all. The comment at lines 204-205 says going on from here should reach 'what other code set it to, where it began', and onwards()'s doc says a count is 'stepped since something else set it' - the fold should stop at the other writer. Any counter that other code wraps or rebases from its own value (tics -= TICRATE, angle &= mask) hits this.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":178},"end":{"line":216},"content_hash":"ebb38f78a8218d67fcab552cca08f66cd70ba999186c63f9e3e1bfba7bb1c0a9"},"suggested_fix":"Once base is another code's store (set(base) with base.step.what === 'store'), stop folding: either return null from splice in that case, or make recurrence() refuse to climb past a role:'before' node whose step is a store by a different pc than the outer store. Add a derive.test.mjs case with an intervening other-pc store made of the held value and assert the count stops there and the other store stays as 'began from'.","summary":"splice folds a count across another writer's reset, dropping that write and producing a wrong count and a wrong 'began from'","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720297Z","id":"f677a4ac3f21e2fbc7e03f797186ad9296e5eca776b28549057f468e12500eea","body":{"detail":"expression() tries both orderings with apply(op, a, b) and prints `${a} ${symbol} ${b}` for the one that matches (lines 355, 408, 417). For rsb apply returns b - a, so the printed text says a - b, the negation of the value. Checked under node: expression('rsb', [0x10, 0x100], {value: 0xf0}) returns '0x10 − 0x100' (= 0xffffff10), and expression('rsb', [0x10, 0x100], {value: 0x3f0, rshift: ['lsl', 2]}) returns '0x10 − 0x100 << 2' (= -0x3f0). crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs decodes Thumb-2 data processing (shifted_operand, immediate), so rsb.w with a register or shifted register reaches this path. Only the immediate form is right, because line 388 special-cases it before reaching apply.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":332},"end":{"line":335},"content_hash":"e94f1cc0b69fe439022d52cf22b96c794fe6d897c2cded78eb1711b2195cd7d9"},"suggested_fix":"Normalise rsb before the generic paths: swap its two operands (and treat it as sub) so apply/print order agree, or change the matching loops to print `${b} − ${a}` when op === 'rsb'. Add the two cases above to the expression test.","summary":"apply() computes rsb as b - a but expression() prints the pair in apply's argument order, so every register-form rsb is printed backwards","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720325Z","id":"13029f79584452bfebe5801f9caad9942cae19ac3598ecd43d605c9a8c631156","body":{"detail":"Line 362 only accepts the order value = v op (c shifted). If the followed value v is the shifted second operand and the constant c is the first (sub.w r0, rC, rV, lsl #2), line 363 rescues only add/adc and line 364 then prints `${v} << n ${symbol} ${c}` without checking it. Under node, expression('sub', [0x10], {value: 0xfc0, rshift: ['lsl', 2], imm: 0x1000}) returns '0x10 << 2 − 0x1000', which comes to 0xfffff040, not 0xfc0. The rule stated in the comment at lines 413-414 is that arithmetic is said only where it comes to the value, and line 364 breaks it whenever c is known.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":358},"end":{"line":365},"content_hash":"cd35ce67df2180356abefe65a8e0391e221881f863e6f22428f50ce8f0c2ed5a"},"suggested_fix":"At line 363, generalise to any op: if apply(op, c, shiftBy(v, kind, n)) === value, print `${hex(c)} ${symbol} ${shifted(v)}`. When c is known and neither order matches, fall back to the words form `${word} (...)` and do not print line 364's unchecked expression.","summary":"with a shifted operand and a known constant, expression() prints an unchecked expression in the wrong order for sub/rsb/bic","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720348Z","id":"9d7cdd61546ebce66d4f01fbc74fe9a8256277e72c26af9c0f8450eabdc9a9c5","body":{"detail":"checks is false when apply(op, 0, 0) is null, and line 419 then prints `a symbol b` without any check. sbc with carry clear: expression('sbc', [5, 3], {value: 1}) returns '0x5 − 0x3', which is 2. A register lsl uses only the bottom byte of the shift register: expression('lsl', [1, 0x120], {value: 0}) returns '0x1 << 0x120'. Line 409 does the same for an immediate with adc/sbc when the carry is set. This breaks the rule in the comment at lines 413-414 that arithmetic is said only where it comes to the value.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":412},"end":{"line":421},"content_hash":"32fc2e0e0b132d57d782e748df59c11fbf15496f52e88f772770fdf7b7816c80"},"suggested_fix":"Add sbc (a - b - 1 or a - b), adc (+0/+1), lsl/lsr/asr by register (b & 0xff, using shiftBy), and sdiv/udiv to apply() so they are checked. Where the carry makes the difference, accept either carry value and print '+ carry' / '− borrow'. Anything left that does not check should fall back to `${word} (...)`.","summary":"ops that apply() does not model (sbc, adc with carry, register shifts, sdiv/udiv) are printed as arithmetic without checking that it equals the value","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720378Z","id":"8077b07a386d3e3f418226e828b933657af1f7f6d27f41bce09f7fe77bda162b","body":{"detail":"OPS maps asr to '>>' (line 35) and the rshift label map at line 350 does the same, and hex() always prints the unsigned value. expression('asr', [0x80000000], {value: 0xf8000000, imm: 4}) prints '0x80000000 >> 4', and the natural reading of that is 0x08000000. Signed multiplies and divides have the same problem: smull's 'high half of 0x10000 × 0xffffffff' (test at web/derive.test.mjs line 47) only equals 0xffffffff if 0xffffffff is read as -1, and the page does not say that.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":33},"end":{"line":35},"content_hash":"3d0426addeb292e62efadfad3a7ce3848846541a722d64c4b2c2bc2b7e1cda2e"},"suggested_fix":"For asr, smull, smlal and sdiv, print an operand with its top bit set as signed (e.g. via valueText, '0x80000000 (-2147483648) >> 4'), or label the operation as signed ('signed >> 4'), so the printed expression evaluates to the shown value.","summary":"asr and lsr both print as '>>' over unsigned hex, so an arithmetic shift of a negative value reads as the wrong number","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720400Z","id":"325652b559c0b46d95eb8997fd4194375b4d1b5ea28dfc2d80a25ff18ecdce45","body":{"detail":"addressExpression returns '0x20000000 + 0x4 << 2' meaning base + (index << 2), and expression() returns '0x903cd7b4 + 0xbb418e45 >> 26' (test at web/derive.test.mjs line 32) meaning a + (b >> 26). In C and JS, + binds tighter than << and >>, so both read as (a + b) << 2 and (a + b) >> 26, which are different values. The inspector shows these right beside the source line (madeNodeHtml in web/inspector.js, the made-src code block), so the C reading is the one a reader will reach for.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":484},"end":{"line":498},"content_hash":"9d2481d986baef3491a94b92878b8fa9cf66feef9d4e23716b36767176f24cdf"},"suggested_fix":"Parenthesise the shifted operand whenever a shift sits next to + or −: `${hex(base)} + (${hex(index)} << ${shift})`, and the same in expression()'s `shifted` helper (line 350) when the outer symbol is +, −, ×+ or ×−. Update the tests.","summary":"printed '+ ... << n' / '+ ... >> n' expressions mean the opposite grouping under C precedence, next to C source lines","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720423Z","id":"d30de4bc895af29f2d805d150daf02523efc85208a9cc139ea5d820f413f2c44","body":{"detail":"times = diff(last, first) / step, where diff is (a - b) | 0. If the true travel is 300 steps of 0x1000000, diff wraps to 44 steps, and count(300*0x1000000+5, 299*0x1000000+5, 1, 0x1000000+5, 5, 1) returns 44 under node. Nothing marks the result uncertain, so the inspector shows 'x44' as fact. Fixed-point angles (BAM) and free-running hashes or positions wrap like this routinely. Separately, step * began at line 228 is compared against a wrapped diff, so a valid run whose first segment wraps is rejected.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":224},"end":{"line":231},"content_hash":"3f097b30a204985ff3065845abdf82bc335d918a3460e82b12d34965457efe11"},"suggested_fix":"Compare (step * began) | 0 at line 228. For the total, use the run's duration (written.run against the steps' period, from the stamps of found and the store) to choose among times ≡ diff/step (mod 2^32/|step|). If that is not possible, set uncounted when |step| * times could reach 2^31, so that a wrapped run is never shown as an exact count.","summary":"count() works out total travel with a 32-bit signed difference, so a run that wraps (or travels more than 2^31) silently gets a wrong count","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720444Z","id":"b3ecea3965058fc15e4fa474d90e757b920f03385b894c8b8b3c36ebed6a0fdf","body":{"detail":"For sub it always computes was = value + other, which is right only for x - c. With the place's own value as the subtrahend (x = c - x, e.g. a toggle 1 - x), operandValues({step:{op:'sub', value:0xf0}, operands:[{step:{value:0x100}}, {role:'before', step:{value:0x10}}]}) returns [0x100, 0x1f0] instead of [0x100, 0x10]. The rshift branch (line 515) likewise always shifts 'other', so when the own value is the shifted operand the result is wrong. Ops not in the table (rsb, orr, mul...) get null and are shown as 'its value before − …', and web/inspector.js:1121 always prints 'its value before' first, so for c - x it gives the wrong order.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":504},"end":{"line":523},"content_hash":"ceac39a59103fefdfa8e4ae824e442bb3ae21504361885046abf496b65a7de6e"},"suggested_fix":"Use the before operand's index i: for sub with i === 1 compute other - value, add rsb (mirror of sub), and apply rshift only to the operand the instruction shifts (index 1). Then verify by recomputing with apply/shiftBy and return null if that does not reproduce value. In inspector.js:1121, print the operands in their real order.","summary":"operandValues works out the 'began from' value assuming it is the first operand, so it is wrong for c - x and for a shifted own value","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:03:25.720468Z","id":"aa086a3506a7b68d08efdbc2286f849b8f5feb08cd3e30a3a1ed9e53264c3f07","body":{"detail":"The chain only needs each link to be an add/sub whose 'before' operand is itself an add. a = b + c + d + e + f compiled as nested adds therefore gives links of length 3 and is shown in web/inspector.js madeNodeHtml as 'stepped 3 times by fn'. The telescoped stepsText still adds up, but the claim that this is a value stepped round a loop is false. Nothing compares the links' pcs, even though the doc comment describes 'position += step round a loop'.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":431},"end":{"line":461},"content_hash":"c02f3d27ba7f6ab8306fd8c537f60ceee4d474047af238d3fbd72e1d83459768"},"suggested_fix":"Require the links to share a pc, or at least for some pc to repeat among the links, before treating the chain as loop steps. Alternatively, only fold a run of consecutive links with the same pc.","summary":"loopSteps calls any chain of 3+ adds 'stepped N times', even straight-line sums made by different instructions","tags":["review","review:derive-2026-10-30","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155449Z","id":"b4893a24fadd4b861fc63aa3cabd629f78affb701c141c8f7e13864d46357438","body":{"detail":"Reproduced with goOn over three canned windows (root store pc 0x100 v=0xa <- add <- held 9 by 0x100; window 1: store 0x100 v=9 <- add <- store pc 0x200 v=8 <- and <- held 0x108 by 0x100; window 2: store 0x100 v=0x108 <- add <- held 0x107). Output: 'value store pc=100 v=0xa rep=2 / value merge v=0xa / before held v=0x107', queries: 3. Mechanism as stated: web/derive.js:206 returns held 0x108 under the inserted 0x200 store; recurrence (web/derive.js:164-166) takes storeAbove = the role:'before' 0x200 store, then climbs to S, and samePlace(S, held) is true (written.by.pc 0x100); splice then overwrites the 0x200 node as target (web/derive.js:192-193). Concrete wrong count and 'began from' shown to the user: blocker kind is right.","kind":"comment","references":"ee4375010de69f0fb24baaa0721e3b55361ce10428502365d886c8af93d4c4e1","summary":"confirmed: goOn folds S across the pc 0x200 writer: final tree 'S=0xa repeats=2 <- before held 0x107', the 0x200 store gone","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155486Z","id":"8602a75caf16f2b035ef9119f0a573a351c8f5ea0be6dd8cd971eaf5f4ee1166","body":{"detail":"web/derive.js:333 apply rsb = b - a; web/derive.js:417 and :355 print `${a} ${symbol} ${b}`. Under node: expression('rsb',[0x10,0x100],{value:0xf0}) -> '0x10 − 0x100'; expression('rsb',[0x10,0x100],{value:0x3f0,rshift:['lsl',2]}) -> '0x10 − 0x100 << 2'. crates/armv6m/src/ops.rs:157 DATA has 'rsb' at index 14, used for shifted-register data processing at ops.rs:184/210, so the two-operand form is reachable.","kind":"comment","references":"f677a4ac3f21e2fbc7e03f797186ad9296e5eca776b28549057f468e12500eea","summary":"confirmed: register-form rsb prints a − b while apply computes b − a","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155498Z","id":"1a58abf0c6a445a314100bdfb49fd92eb27ca49af510125447dc8167d5948a60","body":{"detail":"Under node: expression('sub',[0x10],{value:0xfc0,rshift:['lsl',2],imm:0x1000}) -> '0x10 << 2 − 0x1000' (= 0xfffff040, not 0xfc0). web/derive.js:362 checks only apply(op, v, shift(c)); :363 handles only add/adc; :364 returns without any check.","kind":"comment","references":"13029f79584452bfebe5801f9caad9942cae19ac3598ecd43d605c9a8c631156","summary":"confirmed: line 364 prints an unchecked shifted expression that does not come to the value","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155507Z","id":"dcbbc63f0103635bd8380f1e83716763889b197ee28899a2c47cac65f0632ec7","body":{"detail":"web/derive.js:415 checks=false when apply(op,0,0) is null (apply at :333 has no sbc/lsl/sdiv...), and :419 then joins with the symbol. Under node: expression('sbc',[5,3],{value:1}) -> '0x5 − 0x3' (=2); expression('lsl',[1,0x120],{value:0}) -> '0x1 << 0x120'. :409 likewise prints `${v} ${symbol} ${imm}` for one operand with no value check.","kind":"comment","references":"9d7cdd61546ebce66d4f01fbc74fe9a8256277e72c26af9c0f8450eabdc9a9c5","summary":"confirmed: ops apply() lacks are printed as symbol arithmetic unchecked","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155517Z","id":"14732c36e6abed60cb36b343511104d7500085bb6b3ceb2a6be7b3d0182ffa79","body":{"detail":"web/derive.js:35 asr -> '>>', :350 rshift map asr -> '>>', hex() at :313 prints unsigned. Under node: expression('asr',[0x80000000],{value:0xf8000000,imm:4}) -> '0x80000000 >> 4'. The signed-multiply case is at web/derive.test.mjs:52 (not 47): 'high half of 0x10000 × 0xffffffff' only holds reading 0xffffffff as -1. Concern kind fits.","kind":"comment","references":"8077b07a386d3e3f418226e828b933657af1f7f6d27f41bce09f7fe77bda162b","summary":"confirmed: asr prints as '>>' over unsigned hex, reading as a logical shift","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155527Z","id":"642987da57f9b1cff291b47951036458e00ec7eee2597f8d76ce3c7a456877d5","body":{"detail":"Under node: addressExpression(0x20000010,[0x20000000,0x4],2) -> '0x20000000 + 0x4 << 2' (web/derive.js:494); web/derive.test.mjs:31-33 expect '0x903cd7b4 + 0xbb418e45 >> 26' meaning a + (b >> 26). In C, additive operators bind tighter than shifts (https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/operator_precedence). Shown in web/inspector.js madeNodeHtml (around :1125-1130) via addressExpression/expression.","kind":"comment","references":"325652b559c0b46d95eb8997fd4194375b4d1b5ea28dfc2d80a25ff18ecdce45","summary":"confirmed: 'a + b << n' reads as (a + b) << n under C precedence","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155535Z","id":"16ed7971f1cf9e1c8e8fbac9c2a851aa9774aef09181892af9ba6b3e18af96f0","body":{"detail":"web/derive.js:226 diff = (a - b) | 0; :229 times = diff(last, first) / step. Under node: count(300*0x1000000+5, 299*0x1000000+5, 1, 0x1000000+5, 5, 1) -> 44 (true travel 300 steps). splice sets store.repeats = times - 1 at web/derive.js:202 with no uncertainty mark.","kind":"comment","references":"d30de4bc895af29f2d805d150daf02523efc85208a9cc139ea5d820f413f2c44","summary":"confirmed: count() uses a 32-bit wrapped diff for total travel","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155543Z","id":"bb91ed99062285aeaeb1db47f084905eb8095b62658a4ba33e2d24019cfeea32","body":{"detail":"web/derive.js:517 sub -> value + other regardless of index i; :515 always shifts 'other'. Under node: operandValues({step:{op:'sub',value:0xf0},operands:[{step:{value:0x100}},{role:'before',step:{value:0x10}}]}) -> [256, 496] i.e. [0x100, 0x1f0], not [0x100, 0x10]. web/inspector.js:1121 prints 'its value before <symbol> ...' first when a value is null.","kind":"comment","references":"b3ecea3965058fc15e4fa474d90e757b920f03385b894c8b8b3c36ebed6a0fdf","summary":"confirmed: operandValues assumes the own value is the first operand","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"web/derive.js","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-31T01:05:37.155550Z","id":"705efbd8bec95c811e87a465bf1ee1973aa9bb65c1c2bbdbf72295c995c7c9cb","body":{"detail":"Built a nested add chain ((((1+2)+3)+4)+5), each merge at a different pc (0x102..0x108) with plain held leaves; loopSteps returned 3 links with pcs ['108','106','104']. web/derive.js:432-456 never compares link pcs; web/inspector.js:1112-1115 then renders 'stepped 3 times by'. Suggestion kind fits.","kind":"comment","references":"aa086a3506a7b68d08efdbc2286f849b8f5feb08cd3e30a3a1ed9e53264c3f07","summary":"confirmed: loopSteps folds straight-line nested adds by different pcs into 'stepped N times'","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:27f23ab0-285c-44b4-9dd9-e960c166a6ca"]}}
